;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv]: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv --differentiate]: PASS
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS
;; HOIST-HARDWARE-PROFILE: bmg
;; VERIFY-AUTODIFF: A=4x16@0:0.01 at.A=0,2 expect.A=1.0 atol=1e-2 h=0.5 group=64 output-mat=4x16
;;
;; ENDEAVOUR 175 -- does a GRID-LEVEL atomic accumulation differentiate correctly?
;;
;; This spec exists ahead of grid-reduce-atomic! because it isolates that construct's entire AD
;; question into four lines of kernel.  grid-reduce-atomic! is, in substance:
;;
;;     reduce-workgroup #'+           (already verified, spec 09: 64.0)
;;     then one leader per workgroup:  atomic-add! into a single global cell
;;
;; and if BOTH halves differentiate, the composition needs no VJP of its own.  Worth showing why,
;; because the composition is not obvious:
;;
;;   * the atomic contributes d out / d wg_total = 1, but only in the thread that performed it --
;;     so wg_total_bar = outbar in the leader and 0 in every other thread;
;;   * reduce-workgroup's VJP is an ALL-REDUCE of the adjoint, which then spreads that leader's
;;     outbar to every thread in the workgroup.
;;
;; Composing the two gives xbar_t = outbar for every t, which is exactly right for
;; out = SUM over the whole grid of x_t.  The all-reduce that looked like a curiosity in spec 09
;; is what carries the leader's adjoint back to the threads that fed it.
;;
;; SO THIS SPEC TESTS THE HALF THAT IS NOT YET VERIFIED: the atomic.  out[0] = SUM of A, so
;; d(out)/dA[2] = 1.0 exactly -- the value is independent of thread count, warp width and
;; accumulation ORDER, which matters because atomics do not guarantee order.
;;
;; WHY IT IS NOT ENOUGH THAT IT COMPILES.  It already does.  So did reverse-mode through
;; reduce-workgroup's expansion, which returned 1.0 where the truth was 64.0 (BUG 073) -- a wrong
;; gradient, silently, from a kernel that built and ran.  A cross-thread accumulation is exactly
;; the shape that failure had, and an atomic into a shared cell is cross-thread by definition.
;; The number is the test; compilation is not.
;;
;; output-mat=4x16 RATHER THAN output-vec=1, and the reason is worth recording.  The natural
;; shape here is a length-1 vector -- the design doc specifies grid-reduce-atomic!'s return-vec
;; exactly that way -- and the spec was first written with output-vec=1.  But VERIFY-AUTODIFF does
;; not compute a finite difference for a vector output: it reports `analytical=X expected=Y`,
;; comparing against the directive's own arithmetic rather than a measurement (BUG 078).  That is
;; the weaker test, and it would have made the result below uncorroborated.  With output-mat the
;; harness produces a real FD, which is what let the fix be confirmed by A/B:
;;
;;     rule OFF:  analytical=0.0  numerical=1.0
;;     rule ON :  analytical=1.0  numerical=1.0
;;
;; All 64 threads accumulate into C[0,0], so sum(C) is the grid total and d(sum C)/dA[0,2] = 1.0.
;; Heavy contention on one address is the point, not an oversight: it is the same shape
;; grid-reduce-atomic! produces with one leader per workgroup.

(def-type mat-t (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel diff_atomic_accumulate (A &out C)
  (declare #'(mat-t &out mat-t)
           (global-size :set-to 64)
           (local-size  :set-to 64))
  (let ((tid (to-int (get-local-linear-id))))
    (let ((row (/ tid 16))
          (col (rem tid 16)))
      ;; All 64 threads accumulate into ONE element; sum(C) is therefore the grid total.
      (atomic-add! (~ C 0 0) (~ A row col)))))
